  "conclusions": [
    "Three wide buffer vectors occupy 7924 existing explicit FFs, verified separately in SYN37 and emitted PNR35/49. They are a substantial measured implementation target, not an unexplained weight-count anomaly.",
    "About 8000 FF is an absolute old-storage replacement ceiling; a function-preserving implementation needs new storage/address/control/stream registers. Net FF/LUT savings have not been mapped.",
    "The 7252 distinct direct D-driver LUTs belong to the old wide-buffer implementation; retaining clipping/frame selection/protocol and adding RAM read/address logic means they are not a measured net removal.",
    "input_mem already uses distributed RAM with two real read copies; adding its 4032 source bits to FF savings is wrong.",
    "OLED plus IR account for 788 explicit SYN37 FFs, not thousands of framebuffer FFs. OLED gram is two BSRAMs and has no control-state feedback. IR participates in emergency stop/model fault/wheel enable/rearm feedback and cannot be removed as a display-only feature.",
    "Control has more FFs than u_nav alone but less LUT/ALU/RAM. u_soc is a separate CPU hierarchy. The claim that control is larger than all navigation depends on scope and is false as a blanket resource claim.",
    "No physical CLS estimate for a replacement exists. Six to nine percentage points of 69120 CLS means about 4147 to 6221 CLS; FF/LUT counts do not establish those gains or routing success.",
    "snapshot is a deliberate immutable shift frame to avoid a 168-to-1 24-bit dynamic mux. Replacing it must preserve 168-word frame identity, order, clipping, capture/LOAD timing, reset/fault behavior and busy immutability; direct indexed rewiring can exchange FFs for mux/routing pressure."
  ],
